{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>If you lend silver.</b> In the Mekhilta, Rabbi Yishmael says: every ״אִם״ in Scripture denotes voluntary action, except this one and so forth. And one needs to understand why HaShem would speak in a manner of doubt in a place of certainty. And perhaps it is because the verse comes to inform and to answer as well the one who asks when he sees that a man's honor is greatly increased by abundant gold and his treasuries are filled with massive wealth that he has no need for, and a person might say: ״What are these treasures of gold to a man for nothing? Why did HaShem not suffice to give him the sustenance he requires, and would this one be greater than our father Jacob, who asked (Genesis 28:20) for bread to eat and a garment to wear?״ It is true that what a person lacks beyond his needs has a reason — to chastise him for his sin; but treasuries of wealth — why should a man have them when he has no need for them? Therefore the verse informs that the surrounding matter — that is, those who are not worthy to receive their allotted portion on account of their deeds — is the reason. For HaShem in His kindness gives the necessary abundance generously to each and every man according to his need. And when a person sins and is not worthy to receive his livelihood with honor from the El of Glory, the portion reaching him — the Supreme One does not take it away entirely; rather, it gathers to another place, and that person's livelihood comes through another man to whom that livelihood arrives by way of that place, and he is sustained in lowliness and disgrace as the Decree-Maker decreed in righteousness. And HaShem accomplishes through this manner two good outcomes: the first — that a person be repaid in this world for his evil deed; and the second — so that the giver may merit through the mediation of giving charity and kindness to his fellow." "And this is what the verse means by <b>if you lend silver to My people</b> — meaning, if you see that you had silver beyond what you need for yourself, such that you are lending it to My people, know for yourself that this is not your allotted portion but the portion of others — that is, the poor man — deposited with you, and through this He hinted that you must open [your hand] to him from what is his own. And perhaps He also hinted to him that he should not exalt himself or act superior over the poor man by thinking that he is the one giving to him, and this is what the verse says <b>you shall not be to him as a creditor</b> — in the language of נְשִׂיאוּת (exaltedness) and superiority, for it is from the poor man's own [portion] that he is giving to him. And He returned to say, correspondingly regarding the loan, <b>you shall not impose upon him</b> and so forth:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:37.332461405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}